Virtual Lab Studio
==================

This repository contains two distinct bodies of work.

1. Vendored upstream: `src/virtual_lab/`
---------------------------------------

The directory `src/virtual_lab/` is an unmodified copy of the Virtual Lab
package from:

    https://github.com/zou-group/virtual-lab

Copyright (c) 2026 Kyle Swanson, and the Virtual Lab contributors.
Licensed under the MIT License (see LICENSE).

This tree is vendored verbatim and is deliberately kept byte-for-byte
identical to upstream. Virtual Lab Studio adapts to it; it is never patched
in place. Any change intended for this package belongs in a pull request to
the upstream repository, not in this copy.

`backend/tests/test_upstream_compat.py` enforces this: it fails if any file
under `src/virtual_lab/` is added, removed, or altered. When you intentionally
re-sync with a newer upstream release, update the recorded digest in that test
in the same commit, so the change is explicit and reviewable.

2. Virtual Lab Studio itself: everything else
---------------------------------------------

The rest of this repository — the FastAPI backend under `backend/`, the web
application under `artifacts/`, the shared packages under `lib/`, and the
specifications and documentation under `specs/` and `docs/` — is the Studio
application built around the upstream package. It is a separate work and is
not part of the upstream project.

Licensing
---------

The two bodies of work carry two separate license files, both MIT:

    LICENSE          `src/virtual_lab/` — (c) 2026 Kyle Swanson and the
                     Virtual Lab contributors. Preserved from upstream,
                     unmodified.

    LICENSE-STUDIO   everything else — (c) 2026 Adam Small.

They are kept as separate files rather than two copyright lines in one, so
that the boundary is unambiguous: neither party's notice can be mistaken for
covering the other's code. The upstream LICENSE stays at the repository root
and is never edited, because it is part of the vendored tree's provenance.

Both licenses are MIT, so a downstream user's obligations are the same either
way — retain the notices. The separation is about accurate attribution, not
about differing terms.
